      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+100y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?",
      "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 10\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{10}$ is not a solution.",
